WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.024 --> 00:00:04.134
Can you imagine traveling on the same train for seven days?

2
00:00:04.135 --> 00:00:08.687
If you take the Trans-Siberian Railroad, that is exactly what you will do.

3
00:00:08.688 --> 00:00:19.332
You will pass through eight time zones and speed past two continents, as well as the world's largest lake, traveling from the heart of Europe until you finally reach the Pacific Ocean.

4
00:00:19.333 --> 00:00:26.348
The Trans-Siberian Railway is the longest railway system in the world, stretching over 9,200 kilometers.

5
00:00:26.349 --> 00:00:30.980
It begins in Moscow and extends eastward over the Ural Mountains.

6
00:00:30.981 --> 00:00:40.377
It continues through Siberia, an area which constitutes almost all of northern Asia, to its final destination, the port town of Vladivostok.

7
00:00:40.378 --> 00:00:50.539
Before the Trans-Siberian Railway was built, traveling from western Russia to Vladivostok took weeks—sometimes even months—due to Siberia's harsh climate.

8
00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:57.198
In the extreme north of Siberia, ice and snow can cover the land for up to eleven months of the year.

9
00:00:57.199 --> 00:01:09.234
Weather conditions in the southern part of Siberia, however, are much more hospitable, with average winter temperatures of about -20°C and summer temperatures of 20°C.

10
00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:16.290
This is one of the reasons Russian engineers decided to build the railway in the south; it could be utilized year-round.

11
00:01:16.291 --> 00:01:22.288
Construction of the Trans-Siberian train route began in 1891 and lasted 25 years.

12
00:01:22.289 --> 00:01:29.406
During those years thousands of prisoners, soldiers, and peasants worked through frigid winters and muggy summers.

13
00:01:29.407 --> 00:01:37.383
They began laying track at opposite ends of the country, and the railway was completed when the tracks met in the middle in 1916.

14
00:01:37.384 --> 00:01:45.812
More routes have been added over time, and today the Trans-Siberian Railway connects Russia with China and Mongolia as well.

15
00:01:45.813 --> 00:01:52.153
The Trans-Siberian Railway has become an essential part of the Russian transportation system for several reasons.

16
00:01:52.154 --> 00:02:00.209
For one, it is used to transport 30 percent of the country's exports to the east coast of Russia or to its western borders.

17
00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:08.478
These natural resources and manufactured goods are then sent on to North America via the Pacific Ocean or to Western Europe.

18
00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:14.487
But ordinary people use the railroad as well. It is used by Russians to travel within their country.

19
00:02:14.488 --> 00:02:23.853
It also attracts international tourists who want the unique adventure of traversing all of Asia, watching an entire continent pass by outside their train windows.

20
00:02:23.854 --> 00:02:26.838
Let's take a trip from Moscow to Vladivostok.

21
00:02:26.839 --> 00:02:32.440
Along the way we'll experience the wonders, sights, and sounds of the world's longest train journey.

22
00:02:32.441 --> 00:02:37.780
Traveling on the Trans-Siberian Railway is a great way to meet people from different cultures.

23
00:02:37.781 --> 00:02:42.350
Some travel for short distances, some for the entire seven days.

24
00:02:42.351 --> 00:02:46.271
For one week the train becomes everyone's shared home.

25
00:02:46.272 --> 00:02:54.703
So passengers quickly become friends, sharing food and drinks, telling stories, singing songs, and playing games to pass the time.

26
00:02:54.704 --> 00:02:58.052
There are many trains that use the Trans-Siberian Railroad.

27
00:02:58.053 --> 00:03:01.871
The accommodations available depend on the train you choose.

28
00:03:01.872 --> 00:03:06.426
You can travel in a first-class, second-class, or third-class car.

29
00:03:06.427 --> 00:03:13.756
The first-class cars have cabins for two people, while cabins in second class contain simple beds for four passengers.

30
00:03:13.757 --> 00:03:18.696
Some trains also have third-class cars, which are communal spaces with many bunks.

31
00:03:18.697 --> 00:03:26.959
These cars can get kind of rowdy, especially if a group of soldiers decides to have a party and spend the night playing their guitars and singing.

32
00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:31.709
Every car on every train has two bathrooms, one at each end of the car.

33
00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:38.129
Food is available from a restaurant car, although most people bring snacks with them or buy them on the platforms.

34
00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:45.155
There is an endless supply of hot water from a tank called a samovar, so many people just make noodles.

35
00:03:45.156 --> 00:03:49.086
Each car of the train, regardless of class, has an attendant.

36
00:03:49.087 --> 00:03:55.552
These people are responsible for the comfort and safety of the travelers in their car, so they are very important.

37
00:03:55.553 --> 00:04:01.947
They ensure that people get off at the right stops and make sure passengers don't stay out on the platforms for too long.

38
00:04:01.948 --> 00:04:11.315
Without these attendants, passengers too busy buying local delicacies and souvenirs from vendors might not notice the train leaving and be left behind!

39
00:04:11.316 --> 00:04:15.872
On some trains the attendants are also the people to order some tea from.

40
00:04:15.873 --> 00:04:20.919
Otherwise you'll have to get some hot water from the samovar and make it yourself.

41
00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:24.865
The first part of the journey takes us through European Russia.

42
00:04:24.866 --> 00:04:28.040
The rural landscape in this area is picturesque.

43
00:04:28.041 --> 00:04:36.806
The train passes many small villages where you can see Russian Orthodox churches with their onion-shaped cupolas, and fields with local farm workers.

44
00:04:36.807 --> 00:04:42.020
Before long the train travels into the Ural Mountains, which separate Europe and Asia.

45
00:04:42.021 --> 00:04:46.946
A white obelisk along the route marks the divide between the two continents.

46
00:04:46.947 --> 00:04:51.911
After a day of traveling, we reach the first major stop: Yekaterinburg.

47
00:04:51.912 --> 00:04:58.865
This beautiful city is almost 1,700 kilometers from Moscow and is the capital of the Ural region.

48
00:04:58.866 --> 00:05:05.373
This is also where Russia's imperial family, the Romanovs, was murdered after the Russian Revolution.

49
00:05:05.375 --> 00:05:15.464
There is a memorial to the Romanovs here, as well as many other places to visit, so tourists often get off at Yekaterinburg and stay a few days to sightsee.

50
00:05:15.465 --> 00:05:20.734
From Yekaterinburg we leave the Ural Mountains and continue into continental Siberia.

51
00:05:20.735 --> 00:05:28.317
For the next five days, the train will travel across the taiga and the steppe, which make up most of the Siberian landscape.

52
00:05:28.318 --> 00:05:32.767
Although there are a few stops, the land is largely unpopulated.

53
00:05:32.768 --> 00:05:39.717
Staring out the window of the train at the seemingly endless terrain, you can't help being impressed by its stark beauty.

54
00:05:39.718 --> 00:05:47.299
After two days the train stops at Novosibirsk, the third largest city in Russia after Moscow and St. Petersburg.

55
00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:52.916
It was founded on the banks of the Ob River when work on the Trans-Siberian Railway started.

56
00:05:52.917 --> 00:06:00.383
Now it is home to opera and ballet companies, theaters, museums, art galleries, and one of Russia's best universities.

57
00:06:00.384 --> 00:06:06.057
The next major town on the journey is Irkutsk, which is where you'd disembark to see Lake Baikal.

58
00:06:06.058 --> 00:06:13.167
Lake Baikal is the largest and deepest lake in the world, containing 20 percent of the world's supply of fresh water.

59
00:06:13.169 --> 00:06:18.533
Its crystal-clear waters, wildlife, and breathtaking scenery are well worth seeing.

60
00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:31.693
The lake was too big to lay track around when the railroad was first being built, so for many years, the passengers of the Trans-Siberian had to take a ferry across the lake and switch to another train on the opposite side.

61
00:06:31.694 --> 00:06:36.373
In the winters sleds carried the passengers across the lake's frozen surface.

62
00:06:36.374 --> 00:06:43.723
Later a series of bridges and tunnels were built, and now the tracks curve around Lake Baikal's southern tip.

63
00:06:43.724 --> 00:06:50.182
After three and a half days and about 5,600 kilometers, we reach the town of Ulan-Ude.

64
00:06:50.183 --> 00:06:58.294
This town is very close to Mongolia and is in the heart of Asian Russia. Here the train route splits into three lines.

65
00:06:58.295 --> 00:07:04.749
Trains heading to Mongolia and China begin their journey here, while the main route continues on to Vladivostok.

66
00:07:04.750 --> 00:07:08.915
Trains heading to China, however, cannot simply cross the Chinese border.

67
00:07:08.916 --> 00:07:19.447
Russia and Mongolia both use a wider gauge of railway track than China, so the trains have to have the lower part of their carriages changed, which can take several hours.

68
00:07:19.448 --> 00:07:26.367
The last major stop before Vladivostok is Khabarovsk, which is 8,000 kilometers from Moscow.

69
00:07:26.368 --> 00:07:30.262
From here we finally make our way to the last stop of the line.

70
00:07:30.263 --> 00:07:38.819
After seven days and more than 9,200 kilometers, the train pulls into Vladivostok, Russia's biggest port on the Pacific Ocean.

71
00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:47.677
At the train terminal, a monument marks the last kilometer of the Trans-Siberian Railway, with a total length of 9,288 kilometers.

72
00:07:47.678 --> 00:07:54.519
Until 1991 foreigners were forbidden to visit this city because it was home to Russia's Pacific Naval Fleet.

73
00:07:54.520 --> 00:08:00.182
Even Russians who did not have the proper security clearance needed special permission to enter the city.

74
00:08:00.183 --> 00:08:06.670
Now you can join the many tourists who get their picture taken beside the monument to commemorate their journey.

75
00:08:06.671 --> 00:08:13.719
The Trans-Siberian Railway not only connects Europe and Asia, it also connects the many cultures along its route.

76
00:08:13.728 --> 00:08:20.554
Traveling its thousands of kilometers is the best way to fully experience the beauty and expanse of Russia.

